#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""atl — a token-efficient CLI for Jira and Confluence Cloud.

Built as a drop-in replacement for the mcp-atlassian MCP server. Same operations,
but the shell does the filtering instead of the model, so intermediate JSON never
reaches the context window.

Design constraints, in priority order:

  1. **stdlib only.** No pip install, no venv, no lock file. It starts in ~90 ms
     and it will still run in five years.
  2. **One file.** Vendor it, read it, patch it. There is no framework to learn.
  3. **No resident process.** An MCP server is one process per editor session and
     it lives whether or not you touch Jira that day. This starts, works, exits.
  4. **Compact by default, raw on request.** Every command prints lines meant to
     be read; `--json` gives the untouched API payload for `jq`.
  5. **ADF is built locally.** Atlassian Document Format is what Jira REST v3 and
     Confluence Cloud v2 accept for rich text. Tools that cannot build it lose
     comments, descriptions and page bodies; this one converts Markdown both ways.

Credentials are looked up in this order:

  1. ``~/.atl.json``      {"url":…, "user":…, "token":…, "confluence_url":…}
  2. ``ATL_URL`` / ``ATL_USER`` / ``ATL_TOKEN`` environment variables
  3. ``~/.jira-api-token``  (token only, paired with ATL_URL / ATL_USER)
  4. ``~/.claude.json``   the legacy ``atlassian`` MCP server args, so that people
                          migrating off mcp-atlassian work with zero configuration

Run ``atl init`` to write ``~/.atl.json`` interactively.

The token is read from disk at call time. It is never printed, never logged, and
never passed as a command-line argument, so it does not show up in ``ps``.

MIT licensed. https://github.com/AlexSerbinov/atlassian-cli-for-agents
"""

